WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.759
The grid in front of you is the Mission Catalogue.

00:00:03.759 --> 00:00:11.954
Forty-two missions, from the first robotic probes of the 1960s to the crewed flights planned for next decade.

00:00:11.954 --> 00:00:19.999
Every card has a launch date, a destination, an operating agency, a status, and a one-sentence description.

00:00:19.999 --> 00:00:22.029
Use the filters at the top.

00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:25.863
Filter by status — flown, active, planned, concept.

00:00:25.863 --> 00:00:31.051
Filter by destination — Moon, Mars, Venus, outer planets, deep space.

00:00:31.051 --> 00:00:36.689
Filter by agency — NASA, ESA, JAXA, ISRO, CNSA, Roscosmos, SpaceX, SpaceIL.

00:00:36.689 --> 00:00:38.268
Try this combination.

00:00:38.268 --> 00:00:40.373
Filter status equals failed.

00:00:40.373 --> 00:00:41.727
You'll see Mars 2.

00:00:41.727 --> 00:00:42.704
Schiaparelli.

00:00:42.704 --> 00:00:43.456
Beresheet.

00:00:43.456 --> 00:00:44.133
Hakuto-R.

00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:46.087
Vikram from Chandrayaan-2.

00:00:46.087 --> 00:00:46.614
Hitomi.

00:00:46.614 --> 00:00:50.222
This is the redemption-arc layer of spaceflight.

00:00:50.222 --> 00:00:55.786
Every one of these missions is paired in the catalogue with its successor.

00:00:55.786 --> 00:00:57.816
Vikram CY-2 failed in 2019.

00:00:57.816 --> 00:01:02.853
Chandrayaan-3 landed Vikram's twin at the lunar south pole in 2023.

00:01:02.853 --> 00:01:05.409
Beresheet hit the surface in 2019.

00:01:05.409 --> 00:01:07.665
Beresheet 2 is in development.

00:01:07.665 --> 00:01:12.101
Apollo 1 killed three astronauts on the launch pad in 1967.

00:01:12.101 --> 00:01:16.311
Apollo 11 walked on the Moon two and a half years later.

00:01:16.311 --> 00:01:18.040
Click any mission card.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:29.242
The right panel opens with the full record — launch vehicle, payload mass, crew or instruments, key events, gallery of images, links to read further.

00:01:29.242 --> 00:01:40.069
Where the mission has a destination on a planetary surface, the explore button jumps you to that surface with the landing site already selected.

00:01:40.069 --> 00:01:41.648
Look at the timeline.

00:01:41.648 --> 00:01:42.851
Notice the gaps.

00:01:42.851 --> 00:01:48.790
Between Apollo 17 in 1972 and Luna 24 in 1976, the Moon was visited four times.

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:52.549
Then nothing landed there for nearly thirty years.

00:01:52.549 --> 00:01:59.917
Then Chang'e 1 in 2007 reopened the visit list, and the line is now thicker than it has ever been.

00:01:59.917 --> 00:02:03.075
Forty-two missions is not the whole story.

00:02:03.075 --> 00:02:08.488
Every entry on this page enables the next entry — the capability ladder.

00:02:08.488 --> 00:02:09.992
Filter, click, read.

00:02:09.992 --> 00:02:12.548
See where you stand on the ladder.

